Now I have a clear picture of the vulnerable code. Let me look at the `find_le32` implementation and the `b_info` struct to understand the exact vulnerability.

The vulnerable code is in `PackLinuxElf64::unpack` at lines ~7390-7420:

```cpp
unsigned const N_PEEK(16 * sizeof(int)), H_PEEK(N_PEEK >> 1);
unsigned char peek_arr[N_PEEK];  // 64 bytes
fi->seek(pos - H_PEEK, SEEK_SET);
fi->readx(peek_arr, sizeof(peek_arr));
fi->seek(pos, SEEK_SET);
bool const is_be = ELFDATA2MSB == ehdri.e_ident[EI_DATA];
if (is_be) {
    for (unsigned k = 0; k < N_PEEK; k += sizeof(int)) {
        set_le32(&peek_arr[k], get_be32(&peek_arr[k]));
    }
}
int boff = find_le32(peek_arr, sizeof(peek_arr), size);
if (boff < 0) {
    throwCantUnpack("b_info corrupted");
}
bp = (b_info *)(void *)&peek_arr[boff];

sz_unc = get_le32(&bp->sz_unc);
sz_cpr = get_le32(&bp->sz_cpr);
word3  = get_le32(&bp->b_method);
ftid = bp->b_ftid;
cto8 = bp->b_cto8;
```

So `peek_arr` is 64 bytes. `find_le32` returns the byte offset of the first occurrence of `size` (4-byte LE value) within `peek_arr`. The search range is `0..sizeof(peek_arr)-4` = `0..60`. So `boff` can be 0..60.

Then `bp = (b_info *)(void *)&peek_arr[boff]` and `b_info` is 12 bytes. So if `boff` is e.g. 60, then `bp->sz_unc` (offset 0), `bp->sz_cpr` (offset 4), `bp->b_method` (offset 8) — the last read at offset 8 would be `peek_arr[68..71]`, which is out of bounds of the 64-byte `peek_arr` buffer (which is on the stack).

